    historyContent: {
      origins: "Country/Region of Origin: Texas/Mexico\nTime Period: Late 20th century\nHistorical Development: Developed as a variation of Maltese Cross dominoes. The distinctive 'chicken foot' shape created by playing on doubles gives the game its name.\nCultural Significance: Popular domino game in the southern United States and Mexico. Often played socially in homes and community gatherings.",
      originCountries: [
        'USA',
        'Mexico',
      ],
      timeline: [
        'Late 20th century: Originated in Texas/Mexico region',
        'Evolution: Developed from Maltese Cross dominoes',
        'Modern Era: Popular throughout USA and internationally',
        'Variations: Played with double-9, double-12, or larger sets',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game started with double-9 sets but is often played with double-12 sets for more players or longer games. Tournament rules have formalized some aspects of play.',
      cultural: "The name comes from the visual appearance of the domino layout when a double is played - the three tiles played on it resemble a chicken's foot. The game is often played in boisterous, social settings.",
