          dealing: "Tiles per Player varies by number of players:\n2 players: 12-15 tiles each\n3 players: 10-11 tiles each\n4 players: 9-10 tiles each\n5-6 players: 7-8 tiles each\n7-8 players: 6-7 tiles each\nRemaining tiles form the 'chicken yard' (boneyard)",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Be the first player to play all your dominoes. Score points based on opponents' remaining tiles. First to reach target score (usually 100 or 150) wins.",
          gameplay: "The player with the highest double (9-9 with double-9 set) starts. If not drawn, all players draw one extra tile until found. The first double played starts the 'spinner' with four arms. Subsequent doubles are played crosswise and are 'spinners' - the next three plays must be on the double, creating a chicken foot. After three tiles are played on a double, the arm can be extended normally. Players unable to play draw from the chicken yard. If still unable to play, they pass.",
          keyRules: [
            'Highest double starts (9-9 for double-9 set)',
            'First double is the spinner with four arms',
            'Subsequent doubles are played crosswise',
            'After a double is played, next three plays must be on it (chicken foot)',
            'Chicken foot must be completed before extending other arms',
            'If unable to play, draw from chicken yard',
            'If still unable after drawing, pass',
            'Round ends when player empties hand or game is blocked',
            "Winner scores opponents' remaining pips",
            'First to 100/150 points wins',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Try to play doubles early to create chicken feet and control the board. Get rid of high-pip tiles quickly to minimize points if opponent dominoes. Save tiles that can play on likely doubles.',
          intermediate: "Pay attention to which doubles have been played and which remain. Force opponents to draw by playing doubles when you have tiles to continue the foot. Block opponents by completing chicken feet they can't extend.",
          advanced: 'Sophisticated tile counting to track what opponents hold. Strategic use of doubles to force opponents to pass. Managing hand composition for endgame. Recognizing when to play defensively versus aggressively.',
          tips: [
            "Play the double-blank tile early so you don't get stuck with it",
            'Doubles are powerful - use them strategically',
            "Watch which doubles haven't been played",
            'Try to have a tile that can play on a new double',
            'When an opponent has one tile left, play a double to force them to draw',
            'Keep track of high-value tiles played',
            'Balance completing chicken feet with advancing your own position',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
